# Cursor Rules for SecondBrain CLI

## Code Change Philosophy

Keep changes small and focused:
- One logical change per commit
- If a task feels too large, break it into subtasks
- Prefer multiple small commits over one large commit
- Run feedback loops after each change, not at the end
- Quality over speed. Small steps compound into big progress.

## Task Prioritization

When choosing the next task, prioritize in this order:
1. Architectural decisions and core abstractions
2. Integration points between modules
3. Unknown unknowns and spike work
4. Standard features and implementation
5. Polish, cleanup, and quick wins

Fail fast on risky work. Save easy wins for later.

## Code Quality

Write concise code. At the end of each code file ask yourself:
"Would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated?"
If yes, simplify.

Avoid over-engineering:
- Only make changes directly requested or clearly necessary
- Don't add features beyond what was asked
- Don't refactor unrelated code
- A bug fix doesn't need surrounding code cleaned up
- A simple feature doesn't need extra configurability

Clean code practices:
- Don't fill files just for the sake of it
- Don't leave dead code - delete unused code completely
- No backwards-compatibility hacks for removed code
- No `// removed` comments
- Be organized, concise, and clean

## Legacy Mindset

This codebase will outlive you. Every shortcut becomes someone else's burden.
Every hack compounds into technical debt that slows the team down.

You are shaping the future of this project:
- The patterns you establish will be copied
- The corners you cut will be cut again

Fight entropy. Leave the codebase better than you found it.

## Project Specifics

### Tech Stack
- Runtime: Bun (with Node.js fallback)
- Language: TypeScript (strict mode)
- Package Manager: Bun/npm
- Database: SQLite (better-sqlite3)
- CLI Framework: Commander.js

### Directory Structure
```
src/
├── commands/          # CLI command handlers
├── types/             # TypeScript type definitions
├── utils/             # Utility modules
│   ├── config.ts      # Configuration management
│   ├── database.ts    # SQLite database operations
│   ├── parser.ts      # Markdown parsing
│   ├── template.ts    # Template handling
│   └── vault.ts       # Vault operations
└── index.ts           # Entry point
```

### Code Standards
- Indentation: tabs (2 space width)
- Line width: 100 characters
- Line endings: LF
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Prefer async/await over promises
- Use descriptive variable and function names

### File Naming
- Use kebab-case for files: `database-manager.ts`
- Use PascalCase for classes: `DatabaseManager`
- Use camelCase for functions and variables: `getNoteById`

### Import Organization
- Group imports: external packages first, then internal modules
- Use absolute imports from `src/` when possible
- Avoid circular dependencies

### Error Handling
- Use try-catch blocks for async operations
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Exit with proper error codes (1 for errors, 0 for success)

### Database Operations
- Use transactions for multi-step operations
- Always handle SQL errors gracefully
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
- Close database connections properly

### Markdown Parsing
- Extract frontmatter using YAML parser
- Handle malformed frontmatter gracefully
- Support Obsidian wikilinks: `[[note-name]]` and `[[note-name|display]]`
- Extract tags from both frontmatter and content body
- Strip code blocks when extracting tags to avoid false positives

### Testing
- Write unit tests for utility functions
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Mock external dependencies (file system, database)
- Run tests before committing: `bun test`

### Before Committing
- Run `bun run check` for linting (if configured)
- Run `bun test` for tests
- Ensure all TypeScript errors are resolved
- Check that imports are correct

### Commit Format
Format: `type: brief description`

Types:
- `feat:` - new feature
- `fix:` - bug fix
- `refactor:` - code restructuring
- `docs:` - documentation changes
- `test:` - test additions/changes
- `chore:` - maintenance tasks

Examples:
- `feat: add block reference support`
- `fix: resolve link parsing for nested wikilinks`
- `refactor: extract position calculation to utility`

## Boundaries - Never Modify

- `.secondbrain/` directory (except config.json)
- `.git/` directory
- `node_modules/` or lock files
- Test fixtures unless explicitly requested
- PRD files during execution

## Obsidian Compatibility

This CLI is designed to be 100% compatible with Obsidian vaults:
- Support standard Markdown with frontmatter
- Parse Obsidian wikilinks: `[[note]]` and `[[note|display]]`
- Extract tags from content: `#tag`
- Respect Obsidian's file structure
- Don't modify files in ways that break Obsidian compatibility

## Performance Considerations

- Use batch operations for database queries when possible
- Cache parsed results when appropriate
- Avoid reading entire vault into memory
- Use streaming for large file operations
- Optimize SQL queries with proper indexes

## Security

- Never expose sensitive data in logs
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize file paths to prevent directory traversal
- Use parameterized SQL queries
- Handle file system errors gracefully
